css:
/* WI-002723: the letter is typeset in Readex Pro, with Cairo on the title. Both are
   bundled with the app and inlined by pow_font_faces() - neither is installed on the
   print server, and wkhtmltopdf has no network there.

   The fallbacks after them are not a hedge. If a font file is missing the rules are
   skipped, and without an Arabic-capable face every Arabic word draws as an empty box:
   fontconfig answers an unknown family with Liberation Sans, which carries no Arabic
   glyphs at all. Noto Sans Arabic and DejaVu Sans are what the server does have. */
.pow-letter, .pow-letter * { font-family: 'Readex Pro', 'Noto Sans Arabic', 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif; font-weight: 400; }
/* Normal body text: Readex Pro Regular, 10pt, black - introductory paragraphs and
   table data alike. */
.pow-letter { direction: rtl; font-size: 10pt; color: #000; padding-right: 8px; }
/* The padding is not cosmetic. wkhtmltopdf paints an RTL line a little wider than the box
   it measured for it, and the error grows with the line - so the first letter of the
   longest lines was painted past the right edge of the page and clipped away: "كشف" lost
   its ك and "يعتمد" its ي. It appeared at 10pt and not at the 12px this letter used to be
   set in, because the same lines at a larger size simply reach further. 8px covers the
   worst line on an A4 page; the whole block shifts left by that much, which nothing else
   on the page depends on. */
/* Headings 1 to 6, which on this letter means the section titles and the table
   headers: Readex Pro Bold. */
.pow-letter .sched-title, .pow-letter .nm-title, .pow-letter .pow-tbl th { font-weight: 700; }
/* The English framework, for any Latin run left on the page - today only a client
   whose Full Name in Arabic has not been filled in yet. Body weight is Readex Pro
   Light there; the title keeps Cairo either way. 200 and not 300 because wkhtmltopdf
   buckets font-weight and answers a request for 300 with Regular; the Light face is
   declared at 200 to match. */
.pow-letter .en { font-family: 'Readex Pro', 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif; font-weight: 200; }
/* The masthead is a table because the PDF is drawn by wkhtmltopdf, whose WebKit
   predates flexbox: display:flex was ignored and the title dropped onto its own
   line below the logo instead of sitting opposite it. */
/* WI-002722: the title is Arabic now, so the masthead reads right-to-left like the
   rest of the letter - the logo sits on the right and the title opposite it. */
.pow-hd { width: 100%; border: none; border-collapse: collapse; direction: rtl; margin: 0 0 14px; }
.pow-hd td { border: none; padding: 0; vertical-align: middle; }
.pow-logo { max-height: 64px; }
/* WI-002723: the document title is Cairo Bold Italic at 36pt in black. Cairo ships no
   true italic, so the oblique is the renderer's - which is what "Cairo (Bold/Italic)"
   means for a face that has one weight axis and no slant.

   Selected through .pow-letter so it outweighs the `.pow-letter *` reset above it; on
   its own the universal selector is the more specific of the two and the title would
   silently keep Readex Pro. */
.pow-letter .pow-hd-title { text-align: left; font-family: 'Cairo', 'Noto Sans Arabic', 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif; font-size: 36pt; font-weight: 700; font-style: italic; color: #000; }
.boiler { line-height: 2; margin: 10px 0 18px; }
/* A date or a name is one unbreakable piece. Without this the contract date broke
   after "25 / 02 /" and the client name after "Co.", and each fragment was laid out at
   the far side of a line of its own - the scattered header the client saw. A date is
   written in Arabic now, so it needs the nowrap but not the direction; the client's
   name is the letter's one Latin run and needs both. */
.pow-letter .num { white-space: nowrap; }
.en { direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: embed; white-space: nowrap; }
/* A table value takes its direction from its own first strong character, so an
   Arabic value reads right-to-left and a Latin one left-to-right, in a table that
   is itself RTL. */
.val { unicode-bidi: plaintext; }
/* The letter's one place where a gap has to hold between two Arabic runs: see the
   note beside it in the template. The span sits to the left of the colon in the RTL
   line, so its right edge is the one that needs the room. */
.boiler .cust { padding-right: 10px; }
.schedule { margin: 0 0 14px; line-height: 1.9; }
.sched-title { text-align: center; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px; }
/* No table-layout:fixed and no column percentages: the table sizes its columns to
   their content. Under fixed percentages the Arabic headings shredded into three- and
   four-word fragments, and because no two columns wrapped at the same place the
   days line, the أو and the hours line never lined up across the row. */
.pow-tbl { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; direction: rtl; }
.pow-tbl th, .pow-tbl td { border: 1px solid #000; padding: 6px 8px; vertical-align: top; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
.pow-tbl thead { display: table-row-group; }
.pow-tbl thead th { font-weight: bold; text-align: center; }
/* The headings wrap. Held unbreakable they pinned every column to the width of its
   own heading, and the breakdown - the column with by far the longest text - was left
   with whatever remained: on a contract with several hour figures its lines ran past
   the edge of the cell and the ends were clipped away. The headings are the one part
   of the table that can afford the extra lines; the figures are not. */
.pow-tbl .c-type { text-align: right; }
.pow-tbl .c-contract { text-align: left; }
.pow-tbl .c-worked { text-align: left; }
.pow-tbl .c-breakdown { text-align: right; }
/* A hint, not a lock: the table layout is auto, so a column that needs more than this
   still takes it. It only says where the slack goes, and the breakdown is the column
   that wants it - it carries a line per distinct figure and the rest of the row is a
   single total. Without it the breakdown lines each wrapped onto a second line. */
.pow-tbl .c-breakdown { width: 38%; }
.pow-tbl .ln { margin-bottom: 6px; line-height: 1.35; }
.pow-tbl .ln:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
/* Centred so the separator reads as one, and not as another line of the figures
   it divides - in the headings and in the rows alike. */
.pow-tbl .or { font-weight: bold; margin: 6px 0; text-align: center; }
/* A heading names one unit or two, so its lines need the same spacing the cells use. */
.pow-tbl .hd-ln { line-height: 1.35; }
.empty { text-align: center; font-style: italic; }
.sign { margin-top: 40px; line-height: 2.2; }
.nm-title { font-weight: bold; margin: 18px 0 6px; }
.nm-tbl { table-layout: fixed; }
.nm-tbl .c-nm-cat { width: 70%; text-align: right; }
.nm-tbl .c-nm-amount { width: 30%; text-align: left; }

.nm-tbl .nm-total td { font-weight: bold; }
@media print { @page { size: A4 portrait; margin: 15mm; } }
